    "slug": "sekiro-no-defeat-anime-crunchyroll-2026",
    "pillar": "anime",
    "heroFocus": "center",
    "title": "Sekiro: No Defeat Brings FromSoftware to Crunchyroll",
    "headline": "Hand-Drawn.\nHesitation Is Defeat.",
    "subhead": "FromSoftware's shinobi epic is now a fully hand-drawn anime, streaming on Crunchyroll since September 4. The game-to-anime pipeline just got its best test yet.",
    "description": "Sekiro: No Defeat, the hand-drawn anime of FromSoftware's Sekiro, streams on Crunchyroll from Sept 4, 2026. Sneakz & Beatz on why gamers and anime fans both win.",
    "keywords": [
      "Sekiro No Defeat",
      "Sekiro anime",
      "Sekiro Crunchyroll",
      "FromSoftware anime",
      "Sekiro Shadows Die Twice",
      "video game anime 2026",
      "Sneakz and Beatz",
      "PHRHX"
    ],
    "ogHeadline": "Hand-Drawn. Hesitation Is Defeat.",
    "ogAccent": "7A1F1F",
    "heroCredit": "Qzil.la / Crunchyroll",
    "heroCreditUrl": "https://www.gamesradar.com/new-anime-release-dates-upcoming-schedule/",
    "trendScore": 81,
    "body": "The host lives in the single-player lane, so an anime built from FromSoftware's meanest game belongs on this board. Sekiro: No Defeat premiered on Crunchyroll on September 4, and it's a fully hand-drawn adaptation of Sekiro: Shadows Die Twice from studio Qzil.la.\n\n## What it is\nWolf, the one-armed shinobi, in Sengoku-era Japan, bound to protect the Divine Heir. The game was about posture, deflection and dying a lot. The anime doesn't have a parry button, so the question was whether the tension survives without the controller. Director Kenichi Kutsuna promised a \"truly memorable experience\" for game veterans and newcomers both, and the early episodes are landing with the people who know the source.\n\n## Why hand-drawn matters\nVideo game anime usually cheap out on CG. This one made a point of going fully hand-drawn, and the AI fears that floated around when it was announced got put to rest before the premiere. In 2026 that's a selling point on its own. The sword work reads like the game's deathblows because a human drew every frame of it.\n\n## The pipeline is real now\nEdgerunners proved a game anime could be great. Now there's a Death Stranding anime in the works, Ghost of Tsushima: Legends in 2027, and Sekiro streaming today. The studios that make the games you love are handing the keys to the studios that make the shows you love. That's a good era.\n\n## How to watch\nCrunchyroll, weekly. If you never beat Genichiro, this is the version of Sekiro where you get to win.\n\nHesitation is defeat. Press play.\n\n## Sources\n- GamesRadar - New anime in 2026, including Sekiro: No Defeat (September 4, Crunchyroll): https://www.gamesradar.com/new-anime-release-dates-upcoming-schedule/\n- Crunchyroll - Sekiro: No Defeat: https://www.crunchyroll.com/",
