    "slug": "bungie-destiny-2-red-war-vaulted-content-returning-we-lost-trust-reset-september-21-2026",
    "pillar": "gaming",
    "title": "Bungie: 'We Lost Trust.' Destiny 2 Red War Returns",
    "headline": "Bungie Says 'We Lost Trust'\nand Unvaults Destiny 2",
    "subhead": "Three months after telling players Destiny 2 was done, Bungie's new leadership put out a video and a letter: the Red War and every vaulted campaign, destination and raid are coming back, free, for everyone.",
    "description": "Bungie's new leadership says 'we lost trust' and will restore Destiny 2's vaulted Red War campaign, destinations and raids for all players. No date yet.",
    "keywords": [
      "Bungie",
      "Destiny 2",
      "Red War",
      "Destiny 2 vaulted content",
      "Destiny 2 unvaulting",
      "Bungie we lost trust",
      "Poria Torkan",
      "Marathon PvE",
      "Destiny 3",
      "Bungie reset",
      "Sony Bungie",
      "Sneakz and Beatz",
      "PHRHX",
      "Black-owned media"
    ],
    "ogHeadline": "BUNGIE SAYS 'WE LOST TRUST'\nAND UNVAULTS DESTINY 2",
    "ogAccent": "2E4A7A",
    "heroImage": "https://kotaku.com/app/uploads/2026/09/destiny-2-red-war.jpg",
    "heroCredit": "Bungie via Kotaku",
    "heroCreditUrl": "https://kotaku.com/destiny-2s-red-war-and-other-vaulted-content-is-coming-back-over-six-years-later-2000736168",
    "heroFocus": "center",
    "trendScore": 94,
    "body": "In June, Bungie said Monuments of Triumph would be the last content update for Destiny 2. On September 21 the same studio, with new people at the top, said the opposite out loud. The words matter more than the roadmap, so start there.\n\n## The apology\nThe video is called \"The Next Chapter for Bungie.\" New studio head Poria Torkan and head of product Josh Deane read the room: \"We lost trust, we lost incredible colleagues, and we lost sight of what we should be prioritizing most, our players. As a new leadership team, we must own that.\" Then the line that separates this from a normal PR reset: \"We are not asking you to trust what we say today.\" ([Kotaku](https://kotaku.com/a-humbled-bungie-tries-to-hit-the-reset-button-after-years-of-mistakes-we-lost-trust-we-lost-incredible-colleagues-and-we-lost-sight-of-what-we-should-be-prioritizing-most-2000736178), [Bungie.net](https://www.bungie.net/7/en/News/Article/nextchapterofbungie))\n\nContext for anyone who tuned out: Sony bought Bungie for $3.6 billion in 2022 at the top of the pandemic bubble. Since then it's been revenue misses, repeated layoffs (around 300 cut this summer alone, more than 600 in three years), Marathon on life support six months after launch, and the June announcement that Destiny 2 was getting its final update. A fan petition for Destiny 3 passed 400,000 signatures.\n\n## What's actually coming back\nThe concrete promise: vaulted Destiny 2 campaigns, destinations and raids return to the game, available to all players. That means the Red War, the original 2017 campaign Bungie deleted in 2020 to make room for Beyond Light, plus the planets and raids that went with it. Bungie's own caveat is honest: \"Bringing this content back is not as simple as restoring old files.\" Lighting, encounters, combatants and underlying systems all changed after Beyond Light, so every returning piece has to be rebuilt to today's standard. No date. \"More details in the months ahead.\" ([Kotaku](https://kotaku.com/destiny-2s-red-war-and-other-vaulted-content-is-coming-back-over-six-years-later-2000736168))\n\n## What's not in the letter\nNo Destiny 3. No new expansion. No reversal on Marathon, which gets a PvE mode by December and a note that it \"didn't reach as many players as we hoped.\" Bungie reportedly has nothing new greenlit. So the honest read, per [VGC](https://www.videogameschronicle.com/news/we-intend-to-earn-back-your-trust-bungie-announces-plans-to-bring-back-destiny-2-and-improve-marathon/): restore first, \"new stories and games\" second, and the second part has no shape yet.\n\n## Why a producer should care\nThis is a catalog story. Bungie deleted the first chapter of its own franchise to save server space and shipping time, and six years later the thing the audience wanted most was the thing that got thrown out. Every beatmaker with a hard drive of 2017 sessions knows the feeling. Your old work is your bargaining chip. Don't vault it.\n\n## Sources\n- [Destiny 2's Red War and other vaulted content is coming back](https://kotaku.com/destiny-2s-red-war-and-other-vaulted-content-is-coming-back-over-six-years-later-2000736168) — Kotaku, Sept. 21, 2026\n- [A humbled Bungie tries to hit the reset button](https://kotaku.com/a-humbled-bungie-tries-to-hit-the-reset-button-after-years-of-mistakes-we-lost-trust-we-lost-incredible-colleagues-and-we-lost-sight-of-what-we-should-be-prioritizing-most-2000736178) — Kotaku, Sept. 21, 2026\n- [The Next Chapter of Bungie](https://www.bungie.net/7/en/News/Article/nextchapterofbungie) — Bungie.net, Sept. 21, 2026\n- ['We intend to earn back your trust'](https://www.videogameschronicle.com/news/we-intend-to-earn-back-your-trust-bungie-announces-plans-to-bring-back-destiny-2-and-improve-marathon/) — VGC, Sept. 21, 2026\n",
    "publishedAt": "2026-09-22",
    "goLiveAt": "2026-09-22"
