    "title": "What the Yeezy Split Cost adidas, in Dollars",
    "headline": "What the Yeezy Split\nCost adidas, in Dollars",
    "subhead": "adidas ended the partnership in October 2022 and spent the next two years selling the leftovers. The numbers are the clearest ownership lesson the business has produced.",
    "description": "adidas ended Yeezy in October 2022, took a currency-scale hit and sold inventory through 2024. The dollar figures are an ownership lesson.",
    "keywords": [
      "adidas",
      "Yeezy",
      "Yeezy inventory",
      "Boost 350",
      "adidas earnings",
      "Bjorn Gulden",
      "sneaker business",
      "ownership",
      "2022 termination",
      "North America revenue",
      "Sneakz and Beatz",
      "PHRHX",
      "Black-owned media"
    ],
    "ogHeadline": "WHAT THE YEEZY SPLIT\nCOST ADIDAS, IN DOLLARS",
    "ogAccent": "1A1A1A",
    "heroImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1c/Kanye_West_Saint_Pablo_Tour_TD_Garden_2016_1.jpg",
    "heroCredit": "Wikimedia Commons",
    "heroCreditUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kanye_West_Saint_Pablo_Tour_TD_Garden_2016_1.jpg",
    "heroFocus": "center top",
    "trendScore": 62,
    "body": "Four years ago this month, adidas ended the most profitable creative partnership in modern footwear and discovered it had no plan for the warehouse. What followed — two full fiscal years of guidance warnings, write-down debates, staged liquidations and a North American business in free fall — is the most thoroughly documented answer the industry has ever produced to a simple question: what is a Black artist's brand actually worth to the corporation that distributes it?\n\n## The termination and the hole it left\n\nadidas cut ties in October 2022. The immediate problem was not reputational, it was accounting: hundreds of millions of euros of finished product sitting in warehouses that the company was no longer permitted to sell under the original arrangement. [Retail Dive covered the guidance adidas issued afterward](https://www.retaildive.com/news/adidas-issues-shockingly-bad-2023-guidance-after-ye-breakup/642463/), which was bleak enough to reset expectations for the entire company.\n\nThen came the harder decision. [adidas ultimately chose not to write off most of the inventory](https://www.adidas-group.com/en/media/press-releases/adidas-full-year-results-exceed-latest-expectations-company-decides-not-to-write-off-most-of-its-yeezy-inventory) — a move that acknowledged, in the driest possible corporate language, that the product was still worth more sold than destroyed even with the name attached.\n\n## Selling the leftovers, in installments\n\nThe liquidation ran in waves across 2023 and 2024, with proceeds partly directed to anti-hate organizations. [Sportico reported that 2024 Yeezy sales reached roughly $700 million as the last inventory cleared](https://www.sportico.com/business/commerce/2025/adidas-earnings-2024-yeezy-inventory-1234841876/). Read that figure carefully: a line the company had publicly severed still generated nine figures of revenue two years after the split.\n\nThe cost showed up in the shape of the business. [Sportico also reported North America revenue falling 25 percent in a quarter without Yeezy](https://www.sportico.com/business/commerce/2024/adidas-earnings-q4-north-america-revenue-falls-1234770597/), and [CNBC covered adidas warning on North American sales while the sell-off continued](https://www.cnbc.com/amp/2024/03/13/adidas-q4-2023-earnins.html). One partnership had been carrying a continent.\n\n## The ownership lesson, stated plainly\n\nThe 350 and the 700 were not adidas designs in any meaningful creative sense, and adidas was not shy about how much of its relevance they carried. But the tooling belonged to adidas. The factories belonged to adidas. The inventory belonged to adidas. When the relationship ended, the artist walked away from a product line he could not take with him, and adidas spent two years monetizing shoes his name was on while the two sides argued in public.\n\nBoth parties lost real money. Only one of them retained a manufacturing base, a distribution network and the Boost platform. That asymmetry is the whole lesson, and it is the same asymmetry Michael Jordan avoided only because Nike carved out a separate operating unit in 1997 — and the same one every artist signing a Nike or adidas line today is still signing into.\n\n## What the culture actually took from it\n\nThe immediate read was about politics, and fairly so. The more durable read is commercial: after 2022, the smartest people in this business started asking for equity, for ownership of the mold, for separate legal entities, or they stopped asking and opened their own stores. Terms that would have sounded greedy in 2015 sound like basic risk management now. The Whitaker Group model, the Union model, the independent-brand model — all of them look better after watching a nine-figure creative partnership evaporate on one side and get absorbed into quarterly earnings on the other.\n\n## The take\n\nadidas paid enormously for the split and recovered, because corporations are built to absorb the loss of a person. The artist did not get the mold, the factory, the Boost patents or the customer file. That is not a moral judgment on anyone involved — it is a description of the contract, and it was the contract the whole time. Four years on, the useful thing to take from October 2022 is not the argument. It is the balance sheet: when the relationship ends, whoever holds the inventory holds the business.\n\n## Sources\n- [Adidas 2024 Earnings: Yeezy Sales Hit $700M, Last Inventory Sold](https://www.sportico.com/business/commerce/2025/adidas-earnings-2024-yeezy-inventory-1234841876/) — Sportico, 2025\n- [adidas full-year results exceed latest expectations; company decides not to write off most of its Yeezy inventory](https://www.adidas-group.com/en/media/press-releases/adidas-full-year-results-exceed-latest-expectations-company-decides-not-to-write-off-most-of-its-yeezy-inventory) — adidas Group\n- [Adidas North America Revenue Falls 25% in Q4 Without Yeezy](https://www.sportico.com/business/commerce/2024/adidas-earnings-q4-north-america-revenue-falls-1234770597/) — Sportico, 2024\n- [Adidas warns of falling sales in North America as it continues to sell off Yeezy inventory](https://www.cnbc.com/amp/2024/03/13/adidas-q4-2023-earnins.html) — CNBC, March 13, 2024\n- [Adidas issues shockingly bad 2023 guidance after Ye breakup](https://www.retaildive.com/news/adidas-issues-shockingly-bad-2023-guidance-after-ye-breakup/642463/) — Retail Dive\n",
    "publishedAt": "2026-10-25",
    "goLiveAt": "2026-10-25"
