    "heroFocus": "center top",
    "trendScore": 61,
    "body": "Thirty years ago this fall, a 21-year-old from Hampton, Virginia played his first NBA game in a shoe with a pearlized toe and a question mark on the heel. Nike had the market, the machine and Michael. Reebok had a rookie guard who was too small, too tattooed and too unapologetically himself for the league's marketing department — and it gave him a contract nobody at any brand has matched since.\n\n## The Question was a rookie shoe with no precedent\n\nReebok put Allen Iverson in a signature model before he played a professional minute. The Question Mid ran a pearlized toe cap, a hexalite unit, a translucent outsole and a question mark on the heel — a design that treated an unproven rookie as a franchise. It is still in production three decades later, which is the only performance review that matters.\n\nWhat followed was a run of Answer models that tracked his career: the Answer IV with the shroud he wore through the 2001 Finals, the Que series, the constant retros. [WWD's breakdown of the Reebok deal](https://wwd.com/footwear-news/sneaker-news/allen-iverson-reebok-deal-1237702626/) lays out what the company committed to in exchange.\n\n## The terms, in plain numbers\n\nIverson signed a lifetime arrangement with Reebok — an annual payment plus a deferred trust. [The Philadelphia Inquirer reported that he still receives $800,000 a year](https://www.inquirer.com/philly/sports/sixers/allen-iverson-reebok-lifetime-sneaker-deal-20181015.html) and quoted the company's leadership saying the deal remains worth it. [Bleacher Report covered the $32 million trust](https://bleacherreport.com/articles/2895210-allen-iverson-to-receive-32m-trust-fund-in-10-years-as-part-of-reebok-contract) that he cannot access until he turns 55, which lands in 2030.\n\nTwo things are true about that structure. It is paternalistic — a corporation deciding an adult Black man should not be trusted with his own money until middle age. And it worked: after well-documented financial trouble, the deferred money is intact and the annual payment has kept running for years he has not played a game.\n\n## Why nothing like it exists anymore\n\nThe market consolidated. Nike and Jordan Brand took the top of the basketball endorsement market and never gave it back, and the number of players who get a genuine signature line with development investment behind it has shrunk to a handful. Reebok's basketball program was gutted, sold and rebuilt more than once. [Basketball Network's account of how two Reebok employees identified Iverson](https://www.basketballnetwork.net/old-school/how-two-reebok-employees-discovered-allen-iverson) describes a company willing to make an enormous bet on a player its own executives were uneasy about — the kind of internal fight that a modern brand resolves with a smaller check and a quarterly review.\n\nThe other reason: the deal was made before athlete marketing was fully financialized. A lifetime contract with a deferred trust is a relationship. The current model is a term sheet with performance triggers and an exit.\n\n## What Iverson gave up to get it\n\nHe was the reason the league instituted a dress code. He was fined, lectured and framed as a problem by the same industry that sold his shoes to the kids imitating him. Reebok's willingness to sign that version of him rather than a sanded-down one is precisely why the shoes sold — and the company captured the full value of that authenticity while he absorbed the cultural penalty for it.\n\n## The take\n\nIverson's contract is usually told as a feel-good story about a company protecting a player from himself. Read it the other way: it is the last time a non-Nike brand valued a Black athlete's cultural authorship highly enough to hand him a permanent stake in the business he built. Everything since has been temporary — three-year deals, royalty points, a capsule, a retro cycle the athlete does not control. The Question is still on shelves because a guard from Virginia made a certain kind of Blackness legible and marketable to the entire country. He gets $800,000 a year and a trust he can open in 2030. Reebok gets the silhouette forever.\n\n## Sources\n- [Allen Iverson's Reebok Deal: What Are the Lifetime Contract Details?](https://wwd.com/footwear-news/sneaker-news/allen-iverson-reebok-deal-1237702626/) — WWD / Footwear News\n- [Allen Iverson still gets $800,000 a year from Reebok, and the CEO says it's still worth it](https://www.inquirer.com/philly/sports/sixers/allen-iverson-reebok-lifetime-sneaker-deal-20181015.html) — The Philadelphia Inquirer, October 15, 2018\n- [Allen Iverson to Receive $32M Trust Fund in 10 Years as Part of Reebok Contract](https://bleacherreport.com/articles/2895210-allen-iverson-to-receive-32m-trust-fund-in-10-years-as-part-of-reebok-contract) — Bleacher Report\n- [The story behind Allen Iverson's Reebok deal](https://www.basketballnetwork.net/old-school/how-two-reebok-employees-discovered-allen-iverson) — Basketball Network\n",
    "publishedAt": "2026-11-01",
    "goLiveAt": "2026-11-01"
