    "body": "Michael Jordan played 41 games in the 1998 postseason and wore the Air Jordan 14 in three of them. That is the entire on-court history of the \"Last Shot\": Games 3, 4 and 6 of the Finals against Utah, and one jumper over Bryon Russell with 5.2 seconds left on June 14, 1998. Three games is a smaller sample than most player exclusives get. It is also the only sample that matters, because the shoe was on his feet when he ended the Bulls.\n\n## Built off a Ferrari\n\nTinker Hatfield designed the 14 with Jordan's Ferrari 550 Maranello parked in his head. The Jumpman sits in a Ferrari-style shield on the side, the outsole is shaped like a tire tread, and the vents on the midsole mimic the air intakes on the car. [Andscape](https://andscape.com/features/last-shot-air-jordan-14s-20-anniversary-of-iconic-game-6/) counted the details for the shot's 20th anniversary: an \"air-intake pocket similar to what was found on a Maranello,\" and, the part most people miss, the U.S. Patent and Trademark Office issued Nike design patent No. 400,000 for the sports-car look. Jordan's car became Nike's intellectual property. The 550 itself resurfaced in 2026 when [Joopiter put it up for auction](https://sneakzandbeatz.com/the-lane/michael-jordan-ferrari-550-maranello-air-jordan-14-joopiter-auction-2026), a story already on The Lane, and the \"Blue Ferrari\" colorway that it inspired is [reported to return in fall 2027](https://sneakzandbeatz.com/the-lane/air-jordan-14-blue-ferrari-fall-2027-leak-2026).\n\nThe 14 was not supposed to be a Finals shoe. It was the next season's model, scheduled for a fall 1998 launch, and Jordan pulled it forward the way he had pulled the Concord forward in 1995. The black and Varsity Red pair with the yellow-tinted outsole existed as a sample. He wore it because he wanted to, and the brand built the retail plan around what he had already done.\n\n## The night in Salt Lake\n\nGame 6, Delta Center, June 14, 1998. Jordan stripped Karl Malone on the block, walked the ball up, crossed Russell at the top of the key and hit the pull-up. 45 points. Bulls 87, Jazz 86. Sixth title, second three-peat. The shoe on his foot is the one Jordan Brand has retroed as the \"Last Shot\" in 2005 at $130, 2011 at $160 and 2018 at $190, per Andscape's timeline. The next return is scheduled for May 29, 2027 under 487471-003 at $215 for adults, $165 grade school, $90 preschool and $65 toddler, [Sneaker Bar Detroit](https://sneakerbardetroit.com/air-jordan-14-last-shot/) reports. Same shoe, four retros, price up 65 percent.\n\nAndscape also dug up the strange footnote: within a year of release the 14 was being falsely tied to gang activity, and Nike issued a March 26, 1999 statement denying any connection. A shoe named for a Chicago Bull's final jumper got treated like contraband by people who had never watched the game. That has always been the double life of a Jordan in America: heirloom in one neighborhood, suspicion in the next.\n\n## \"Last\" is a marketing word\n\nThe name has a problem, and everyone who buys the shoe knows it. Jordan came back in 2001 with the Wizards and took a few thousand more shots, so the \"last shot\" was the last shot as a Bull, not the last shot of his career. Jordan Brand does not clarify. The name sells the ending people wanted, the one Spike Lee's \"The Last Dance\" cut in 2020 reinforced, and the retro schedule treats June 14 as a sacred date even when the release lands in May. Sneaker Bar Detroit's read on the 2027 date is that it lines up with no anniversary at all; it lines up with demand.\n\n## Who owns the moment\n\nJordan's jumper belongs to the NBA's footage library. The shoe design belongs to Nike under patent 400,000. The car belonged to Jordan, until the auction. The name belongs to Jordan Brand's marketing. Jordan gets a royalty on every pair, which is the best deal any Black athlete has ever cut with a shoe company, and it is still a royalty on someone else's asset. Bryon Russell, who is in the photograph, gets nothing and has been asked about the push-off for 28 years.\n\n## The take\n\nThe Last Shot 14 is a three-game shoe carrying a 28-year business. That is not a knock; it is the whole model. Jordan Brand's catalog is a set of moments with leather attached, and the 14 is the purest example because it has the fewest games and the biggest moment. The lesson for the next generation of signature athletes is simple: you do not need a season, you need one shot, a camera, and a company that will patent the shoe you took it in.\n\n## Sources\n- [On the 20th anniversary of an iconic Game 6, six facts about the 'Last Shot' Air Jordan 14s](https://andscape.com/features/last-shot-air-jordan-14s-20-anniversary-of-iconic-game-6/) — Andscape, June 13, 2018\n- [Air Jordan 14 \"Last Shot\" 2027](https://sneakerbardetroit.com/air-jordan-14-last-shot/) — Sneaker Bar Detroit, July 8, 2026\n- [Five Facts You Need to Know About the Air Jordan 14](https://www.sneakerfreaker.com/features/five-facts-you-need-to-know-about-the-air-jordan-14) — Sneaker Freaker, September 16, 2021\n- [This is How The Ferrari 550 Directly Inspired One of Michael Jordan's Sneakers](https://www.autoblog.com/features/this-is-how-the-ferrari-550-directly-inspired-one-of-michael-jordans-legendary-sneakers) — Autoblog, September 8, 2026\n",
