    "body": "André 3000 and Big Boi are taking a 28-year-old Oakland producer to federal court over five letters. The ATLiens are suing Ovrkast.\n\n## What was filed\n[@Kurrco](https://x.com/Kurrco/status/2102477743099093476) put the complaint on timelines Tuesday, September 22. [XXL](https://www.xxlmag.com/outkast-sues-ovrkast-similar-rap-name/) pulled the court documents the same day. The complaint was filed September 16 in federal court in Georgia by High Schoolers LLC, the company that holds OutKast's trademarks. The defendant is Silas Wilson, the East Oakland rapper and producer who records as Ovrkast. (lowercase, period included). There are three claims: trademark infringement, unfair competition and breach of contract.\n\nThe filing calls Wilson's use of the name \"improper, willful, unauthorized, and illegal.\" According to [The Source](https://thesource.com/2026/09/22/outkast-takes-ovrkast-to-court-after-rapper-misses-agreed-name-change-deadline/), it asks a judge to bar him from using OVRKAST on music, merch, websites and social pages. It also seeks the profits tied to the name, plus damages. [Stereogum](https://stereogum.com/2512266/outkast-sue-ovrkast/news), citing KQED, reports that OutKast wants a jury trial.\n\n![Ovrkast.](https://lede-admin.stereogum.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/64/2026/09/unnamed-2026-09-22T170650.376.png \"Julian Edward via Stereogum|https://stereogum.com/2512266/outkast-sue-ovrkast/news\")\n\n## The deal he allegedly didn't keep\nThis isn't a cold-open lawsuit. According to the complaint, OutKast's lawyers sent a cease-and-desist in June 2025. The two sides then negotiated a settlement in which Wilson would rebrand as **OVERKXST**. The complaint says he never finished the switch.\n\n**The deadline depends on who you read.** Kurrco, The Source and Stereogum say the switch was due by July 15, 2026. [VICE](https://www.vice.com/en/article/outkast-sues-ovrkast-after-rapper-admits-fans-keep-confusing-their-names/) has a different timeline. It says the change was due by June 15, that Wilson kept using the name past June 25, and that OutKast sent a breach notice on August 5 with an August 11 deadline to comply. VICE also reports that Wilson pitched two other replacement names and OutKast rejected both. We haven't seen the complaint ourselves, so treat the exact dates as unconfirmed. Every outlet agrees on the basic story: a name change was agreed to, the deadline passed, and a lawsuit followed.\n\n## The quote that's doing the heavy lifting\nOutKast's best piece of evidence comes from Wilson himself. In a 2025 [Rolling Stone interview](https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-features/no-filler-indie-rap-ovrkast-1235380963/), he complained that people keep reading his name as Outkast. The complaint quotes that interview as proof of confusion. Likelihood of confusion is the core question in any trademark case, and his own complaint about being mixed up is now OutKast's exhibit.\n\nHe has always said the name comes from \"overcast,\" the cloudy-day mood of his beats, not from Atlanta. That may be true. It doesn't change what's on paper.\n\n## His response\nWilson answered on socials Tuesday. On X, he wrote \"Ouch.\" On Instagram, he tagged both members: \"I thought it was love @andre3000 @bigboi (yes this is real).\" In the comments he added that he's \"jus a young black man trying to get ahead.\" Per VICE, the comments are mostly on his side, with fans saying they've never mixed the two up. Neither André nor Big Boi has said anything publicly. XXL says it has reached out to Wilson's team.\n\n!youtube(https://youtu.be/EaD_v3G6LOg)\n\nIf you only know the name from this week, here's the actual work. \"Strange Ways\" with Vince Staples is his most-watched video. His catalog goes back to the Lo-Fiction crew in East Oakland. He produced \"EL TORO COMBO MEAL\" on Earl Sweatshirt's *Feet of Clay*, and he has two Pitchfork-reviewed projects, *Try Again* and the *RESET!* EP. He's a respected underground producer, not someone trading on OutKast's name.\n\n## This is a pattern, and that's the point\nHigh Schoolers LLC has done this before. It sued the masked EDM duo ATLiens over the title of OutKast's 1996 second album, and that case settled out of court in February 2026. OutKast hasn't released a record in about twenty years. André's latest album was a flute record. But the name went into the Rock & Roll Hall of Fame last November, and whoever manages the brand is protecting it the way labels protect a catalog.\n\n![OutKast](https://www.vice.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/09/Web_Photo_Editor-2026-09-22T164412.266.jpg?w=1200 \"Eric Thayer / Los Angeles Times via Getty Images, via VICE|https://www.vice.com/en/article/outkast-sues-ovrkast-after-rapper-admits-fans-keep-confusing-their-names/\")\n\n## The take\nThe online reaction has been easy: rap is built on bent spellings, and two legends are going after a kid from the Town. That's half the story. The other half is that Wilson allegedly agreed to become OVERKXST, and the deadline passed anyway. On the facts, it's hard to see how he wins. The complaint has his own quote about confusion and an agreement he's accused of breaking.\n\nThe cultural question is harder. Southern rap's biggest names are going after a Black independent producer who built his name on Bandcamp and SoundCloud. Every artist today is told to own their name, their masters and their IP. OutKast owns theirs, and this is what enforcing that ownership looks like when the other artist is small. Hip-hop has always celebrated ownership, but it doesn't usually look at who pays when it's enforced against someone smaller.\n\nThe likely ending is a settlement, a new spelling and, eventually, streaming profiles that update. What could change that is if André or Big Boi responds to \"I thought it was love\" personally.\n\n## Sources\n- [@Kurrco on X](https://x.com/Kurrco/status/2102477743099093476), September 22, 2026\n- [XXL, \"OutKast Sues Ovrkast. for Having Similar Rap Name, Ovrkast. Feels Hurt by Lawsuit\"](https://www.xxlmag.com/outkast-sues-ovrkast-similar-rap-name/), September 22, 2026\n- [Stereogum, \"Outkast Sue Ovrkast\"](https://stereogum.com/2512266/outkast-sue-ovrkast/news), September 22, 2026\n- [The Source, \"OutKast Takes Ovrkast to Court After Rapper Misses Agreed Name-Change Deadline\"](https://thesource.com/2026/09/22/outkast-takes-ovrkast-to-court-after-rapper-misses-agreed-name-change-deadline/), September 22, 2026\n- [VICE, \"Outkast Sues Ovrkast After Rapper Admits Fans Keep Confusing Their Names\"](https://www.vice.com/en/article/outkast-sues-ovrkast-after-rapper-admits-fans-keep-confusing-their-names/), September 22, 2026\n- [Rolling Stone, Ovrkast interview](https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-features/no-filler-indie-rap-ovrkast-1235380963/), 2025",
    "heroImage": "https://townsquare.media/site/812/files/2026/09/attachment-ovrkast-outkast-header.jpg?w=1200&format=natural",
    "publishedAt": "2026-09-22",
    "goLiveAt": "2026-09-22"
