    "heroCreditUrl": "https://www.gamesradar.com/games/grand-theft-auto/gta-6s-usd1-million-billboard-goes-up-despite-protests-from-local-police-as-rockstars-real-life-miami-takeover-gets-underway/",
    "heroFocus": "center",
    "trendScore": 96,
    "body": "Rockstar didn't drop a trailer this week. It dropped a sign on top of an NBA arena.\n\n## A helicopter and a roofline\nOn September 22, crews lifted a \"Welcome to Vice City\" sign onto the Kaseya Center, home of the Miami Heat, piece by piece, with a helicopter doing the heavy work. People watched it go up on live city cams. It's the first physical piece of Rockstar's Miami push, and it went up eight weeks before *Grand Theft Auto 6* launches on November 19. ([GamesRadar](https://www.gamesradar.com/games/grand-theft-auto/gta-6s-usd1-million-billboard-goes-up-despite-protests-from-local-police-as-rockstars-real-life-miami-takeover-gets-underway/))\n\n## The number\nThe Miami Herald, via GamesRadar, reports Miami-Dade County collects $975,000 for this one sign. Forbes says it stays up until April 2027. Miami Beach and the City of Miami have their own separate deals with Rockstar, so this is the opening shot, not the whole campaign. ([Forbes](https://www.forbes.com/sites/brianmazique/2026/09/24/rockstar-just-spent-nearly-1-million-to-turn-miami-into-vice-city/))\n\n## The pushback\nSome local police and the Miami-Dade sheriff objected to a county building advertising a crime game. Officials approved it anyway. That argument is as old as the series. The difference now is that the government cashing the check is the same one complaining about the content.\n\n## What it tells you about November\nRockstar has kept this campaign quiet and controlled, drip by drip. Now it's renting the skyline of the city it fictionalized. When a company is this confident, it stops buying ads and starts buying landmarks. Same week, Rockstar opened pre-orders on a $400 Vice City merch box that doesn't even include the game. They know you're coming.\n\n## The culture read\nMiami gave the world Luke, Trick Daddy, bass music, and the neon look every game studio has copied since 2002. Rockstar built Vice City out of that. Now the city is getting paid for the reference, at least a little. The people who made Miami feel like Miami should be first in line when the checks go out.\n\nBuilt for the culture. Operated by PHRHX through Sneakz & Beatz LLC. Black-owned, four pillars: sneakers, hip-hop, anime, gaming.\n\n## Sources\n- [GamesRadar, Sept. 2026](https://www.gamesradar.com/games/grand-theft-auto/gta-6s-usd1-million-billboard-goes-up-despite-protests-from-local-police-as-rockstars-real-life-miami-takeover-gets-underway/)\n- [Forbes, Sept. 24, 2026](https://www.forbes.com/sites/brianmazique/2026/09/24/rockstar-just-spent-nearly-1-million-to-turn-miami-into-vice-city/)\n- [VGC, Sept. 2026](https://www.videogameschronicle.com/news/grand-theft-auto-6-is-getting-a-400-box-of-merch-with-11-items-including-a-figure-sunglasses-and-new-era-hat/)\n",
